# Do not identify the author

Do not name the author's home, starting county, city, or the set of states they personally asked to research in the README, other user-facing docs, commit messages, or chat replies.

Do not keep a named county as "the sample" if that name identifies the author. Describe it as one unnamed county.

Do not list a handful of states as the levy states. In the README and in any global map sentence, describe every state the same way: a published levy stack when one is loaded, otherwise the Census median. A sentence about the place currently on screen may name that place and the file that covers it.

The map opens on the country. Do not center the first view on the starting place.

The rate skill may record a state's formula, source, and known bad shape. It must not name the starting county or city. Its description must not list a handful of states as the places that were requested.

A GitHub issue reporting an actual rate is not applied automatically.

## How to say it

Bad: a real county named as "the detailed sample," a short list of states called out as the levy states, or a global legend that names only those states.

Good: "One unnamed county has district polygons." "Where a published levy stack is loaded, the map uses it. Otherwise it uses the Census median."

Do not repeat a stripped place name in neutral product language, in a claim that the name will be removed, or in instructions to another agent. A README edit does not replace this rule file. When this rule is wrong, change this file in the same turn.
